---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/selenium`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Selenium Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Selenium', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Selenium: Thyroid, Brazil Nuts, and the Narrowest Nutrient Safety Margin"
  description="Selenium reference — RDA, thyroid function, Hashimoto's TPO evidence, the SELECT prostate cancer trial, brazil nut dosing, and how easy it is to overshoot."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/selenium", label: "Selenium" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Selenium</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Selenium is a trace mineral incorporated into selenoproteins — most importantly
      glutathione peroxidase (antioxidant defence), iodothyronine deiodinases (thyroid
      hormone activation), and thioredoxin reductases. The gap between adequate and toxic
      intake is one of the narrowest of any nutrient.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adults:</strong> 55 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 60 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Lactation:</strong> 70 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Tolerable Upper Intake Level:</strong> 400 µg/day — this is roughly 7× the RDA, the tightest safety margin in the fat- and water-soluble vitamin/mineral list.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Thyroid — Hashimoto's and Graves'</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The thyroid gland has the highest selenium concentration of any organ in the body.
      Multiple RCTs of selenomethionine 200 µg/day for 3–12 months in Hashimoto's
      thyroiditis show a modest but significant reduction in TPO antibody titres. Whether
      that translates to reduced hypothyroidism progression or clinical benefit is less
      clear. In Graves' orbitopathy, selenium 200 µg/day BID for 6 months improved eye
      symptoms and quality of life in a multicentre European trial.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cancer — the SELECT surprise</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The 35,000-man SELECT trial (2011) tested selenium (200 µg) and/or vitamin E for
      prostate cancer prevention. Not only did selenium not prevent cancer, it may have
      increased type 2 diabetes risk in men with high baseline selenium. This overturned a
      decade of enthusiasm from earlier smaller trials and is a good example of why single
      positive trials don't warrant supplementation across the general population.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Toxicity — selenosis</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Chronic intake &gt; 400 µg/day causes selenosis: garlic breath, brittle/lost hair and
      nails, GI upset, and peripheral neuropathy. High-dose supplement mishaps have caused
      acute poisonings. Because content varies wildly, brazil nuts can push you into
      excess: a single large nut ranges from 68 to 91 µg, and one poorly sourced batch
      analysed at 500+ µg per nut. Two brazil nuts a day is a sensible ceiling.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Brazil nuts (1 oz, 6–8 nuts): 544 µg — highest of any food; wildly variable</li>
      <li>Tuna, yellowfin (3 oz): 92 µg</li>
      <li>Halibut (3 oz): 47 µg</li>
      <li>Sardines (3 oz): 45 µg</li>
      <li>Turkey (3 oz): 31 µg</li>
      <li>Beef (3 oz): 33 µg</li>
      <li>Egg (1 large): 15 µg</li>
      <li>Brown rice (1 cup cooked): 19 µg</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Selenium status can be measured (plasma or
      erythrocyte selenium) if there's a specific clinical reason. Don't stack a multivitamin
      containing 55 µg with additional selenium capsules and daily brazil nuts.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/selenium" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
